WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
For discrete variables, Bayesian networks often use conditional probability tables,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.110
or CPTs. A discrete variable has a set of named states, such as true

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.660
or false, high or low, working or failed.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.990
A root node has no parents, so it uses a prior probability distribution.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.750
For example, Weather might have probabilities for sunny, cloudy,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.945
and rainy before considering any parent variable.

00:00:18.945 --> 00:00:22.040
A child node has probabilities conditioned on its parents.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.520
Suppose a simple network has Weather and Sprinkler as parents of Wet Ground.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.680
The probability of Wet Ground depends on whether the weather is rainy and whether the

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.105
sprinkler is on.

00:00:30.105 --> 00:00:33.935
The CPT for Wet Ground has a row for each relevant combination of parent states.

00:00:33.935 --> 00:00:36.580
Each row should form a valid probability distribution.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:37.590
In a true-or-false row,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.199
the probability of true plus the probability of false should sum to one.

00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:44.920
The graph lets the joint probability distribution be factored into local terms.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.005
One compact way to write the idea is:

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:50.490
the probability of all variables together equals the product of each variable's

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.080
probability given its parents.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:52.590
In words,

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.480
instead of building one enormous table that lists every possible state combination,

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.790
the Bayesian network breaks the model into smaller local relationships.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.260
That is one reason Bayesian networks are useful for practical modeling.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.040
Factorization is not magic compression.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.840
It depends on the conditional independence assumptions in the graph.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.790
If the graph leaves out an important dependency, the factorized model can be misleading.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:12.320
Also,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.580
not every Bayesian network uses simple CPTs. Continuous or hybrid networks may use

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:17.750
Gaussian distributions,

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.710
regression-style conditional models, or other local probability distributions.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:25.710
The core idea remains the same: local distributions, organized by the graph,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.720
define the larger probabilistic model.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.760
In practice, CPT design is often a modeling bottleneck.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.085
Too many parents can create a table with many rows,

00:01:33.085 --> 00:01:35.940
and each row needs probability values that mean something.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.535
Modelers sometimes simplify states, combine variables,

00:01:38.535 --> 00:01:42.075
or use structured conditional distributions to keep the model manageable.

00:01:42.075 --> 00:01:44.395
The goal is not to make the largest possible table.

00:01:44.395 --> 00:01:48.075
The goal is to represent the relationships at the level of detail needed for the reasoning

00:01:48.075 --> 00:01:48.580
question.
